<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 13 Practice</title>

    <p>
        <em>Given a conditional statement, identify the
                            hypothesis and conclusion, determine its truth
                            value, and apply it.
        </em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know:</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        In a conditional statement the hypothesis is the part
                        after the if, not including the if, and the conclusion
                        is the part after the then, not including the then.
                        In <q>If <m>x</m> is even then <m>x^{2}</m> is even</q>,
                        the hypothesis is <q><m>x</m> is even</q> and the
                        conclusion is <q><m>x^{2}</m> is even</q>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Modus ponens: If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true and <m>P</m>
                        is true then <m>Q</m> is true.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Modus tollens: If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true and
                        <m>Q</m> is false then <m>P</m> is false.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true, and you know <m>P</m> Is
                        false or <m>Q</m> is true, you can't conclude anything.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The truth table for <m>P\implies Q</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice</title>

        <p/>
        <exercise>
            <task>
                <p>
                    Weekly Practice 10 #1
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p>
                    Worksheet pages 77-78
                </p>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the conditional statement ``If <m>n</m> is a prime
                    number then <m>n^{2}</m> has exactly three positive factors.
                    
                    Using ONLY that the conditional statement is true, and each
                    of the following (individually) what can you conclude (if
                    anything)?
                    Explain your answer.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the hypothesis and what is the conclusion?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The hypothesis is <q><m>n</m> is prime</q>. The conclusion is 
                        <q> <m>n^{2}</m> has exactly three positive factors</q>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>3</m> is prime,
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Since <m>3 </m> is prime, then <m>3^2=9</m> has exactly
                        three positive factors. Here, the hypothesis is true for <m>n=3</m>
                        and so the conclusion must also be true.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>6</m> is not prime,
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        In this case, the hypothesis is false, but we cannot conclude 
                        whether the conclusion is true or false. We can see this
                        in the truth table, we have a true conditional statement, so 
                        we consider only the first, third, and fourth row. But the third
                        and fourth row have the hypothesis is false, but the conclusion can 
                        be true or false.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>5^{2}</m> has 3 positive factors
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        In this case, the conclusion is true, so we cannot make
                        a conclusion using only the conditional statement. 
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>4^{2}</m> has 5 positive factors
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        In this case, the conclusion is false, so by the 
                        contrapositive <m>4</m> is not prime. Note here
                        we've also narrowed down to one row of the truth table.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the following conditional statement <q>If the
                    function <m>y=f(x)</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then
                    <m>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)</m> exists.</q> 
                    Using ONLY that the conditional statement is true, and each
                    of the following (individually) what can you conclude (if
                    anything)?
                    Explain your answer.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is the hypothesis
                    and what is the conclusion?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>  
                    <p>
                        The hyptohesis is <q>the function <m>y=f(x)</m> is continous at <m>x=a</m>.   </q> 
                        The conclusion is <q><m>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)</m> exists.</q>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)</m> is a function that is continuous at <m>x=-3</m>
                </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The hypothesis is true for <m>f(x)</m> and <m>x=-3</m> and so 
                        the conclusion must also be true (as the conditional statement is true). Thus 
                        <m>\lim_{x\to -3} f(x)</m> exists. 
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>g(x)</m> is a function that is not continuous at
                    <m>x=7</m>
                </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Here the hypothesis is false, and so no conclusion can be made.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                <p>
                    for a function <m>h(x)</m>, <m>\lim_{x\to 17}h(x)</m> exists
                </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Here the conclusion is true, but in that case the hypothesis
                        could be true or false. So no conclusion can be made.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                <p>
                    for a function <m>k(x)</m>, <m>\lim_{x\to \pi}k(x)</m> does
                    not exist
                </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Here the conclusion is false, so we can conclude the hypothesis is 
                        false (using the contrapositive, or modus tollens, or the truth table).
                        Thus, M<m>k(x)</m> is not continuous at <m>x=\pi</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
</handout>